{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}MRC Hinterlands — Rules{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<h1 class="mb-4">MRC Hinterlands League — Rules Summary</h1>
<p class="text-muted mb-5">This is a summary of the core rules from the MRC Hinterlands League rulebook. Full rules are a supplement to <em>Hot Spots: Hinterlands</em>. Only pages 1–8 are essential for league play; the rest are additional options.</p>

<div class="row g-4">

    {# Building Your Force #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Building Your Force</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>Select a faction from the Master Unit List (<a href="https://www.masterunitlist.info" target="_blank">masterunitlist.info</a>) and choose starting units from that faction's MUL.</li>
                    <li>Start with <strong>3000 BV</strong> to spend and <strong>3000 Support Points (SP)</strong>.</li>
                    <li>You must select at least one Mech. You may add a second Mech, a vehicle, or two Battle Armor units.</li>
                    <li>No aerospace units, no AE-damage units (artillery, Arrow IV), no Unique units.</li>
                    <li>All units must come from the same IlClan faction MUL. Experimental tech is allowed.</li>
                    <li>Each unit starts at <strong>Gunnery 4 / Piloting 5</strong> with <strong>150 XP</strong> to allocate. Battle Armor squads get 75 XP each.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# League Play Structure #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">League Play Structure</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Each month a <strong>Hot Spot contract</strong> is posted in Discord. Players pair up, choose one of the two opposing contracts, and complete it. Alternatively, roll a random contract via Dobless Information Services or the MRC Contract Generator (p. 15).</p>
                <p>A company may only receive credit for <strong>one contract per month</strong>.</p>
                <dl class="row mb-0">
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Downtime</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">After each contract, players receive one month of downtime at a Hiring Hall of their choice. Downtime at a Hiring Hall costs a flat <strong>50 SP</strong> regardless of scale.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Overtime</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">If contracts for the month are complete, players may use the <em>Taking One for the Team</em> rules (HS:H p. 38) to run additional contracts with a new mercenary company.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Responsibilities</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Players maintain their own Force record sheets. Incomplete contracts reset all accrued damage, salvage, and payments.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Reputation & Salvage #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Reputation &amp; Salvage</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>All companies start with <strong>Reputation 1</strong>. Winning a full contract (at least half the tracks) gains +1 reputation. You never lose reputation for <em>losing</em> a contract, only for breaching it (–3) or abandoning objectives.</p>
                <p><strong>Spending Reputation:</strong> Spend 1 reputation to shift a RAT result by ±1, or to negate a Command Complication roll. You must keep at least 1 reputation to spend.</p>
                <p><strong>Salvage</strong> is the right to claim destroyed or crippled enemy units left on the field after a track. The victor may claim salvage if they successfully complete the track. Salvaged mechs are in their end-of-battle condition and must be repaired. If you decline salvage, the original owner may ransom the unit back.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Scale Limitations & Combat Pay #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Scale Limitations &amp; Combat Pay</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>The force you may deploy per track is capped by the contract's scale. In this league, the limit is <strong>3000 BV and 2 units per scale</strong>. Unit limits are checked once at the start of each contract.</p>
                <p>Infantry (battle armor, conventional) may be fielded at 2-for-1 against other unit slots, but this does not bypass the BV cap.</p>
                <p><strong>Maintenance:</strong> 500 SP × contract scale per month, paid regardless of whether a track is played.</p>
                <p><strong>Combat Pay</strong> is paid at the end of each track (500 SP × track scale):
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li>No objectives achieved → no pay</li>
                    <li>At least one objective, fewer Victory Points → half pay (250 × scale)</li>
                    <li>More Victory Points than opponent → full pay (500 × scale)</li>
                    <li>All objectives achieved → half-again pay (750 × scale)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Force Scale Table #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Force Scale</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>The force scale determines the maximum force size and associated costs.</p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Scale</th><th>Force</th><th>BV</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td>Mixed Lance of 'Mechs and Support</td><td>3,000 BV</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Mixed Company</td><td>9,000 BV</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Reputation Progression #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Reputation Progression &amp; Support Rating</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Your company's reputation determines your force scale and military rank as you grow:</p>
                <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Reputation</th><th>Scale</th><th>Rank</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>0–2</td><td>1</td><td>Sergeant / Point Commander</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3–10</td><td>2</td><td>Lieutenant / Star Commander</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>11–20</td><td>3</td><td>Captain / Star Captain</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Support Rating</strong> modifies contract terms based on your company's standing. A Support Rating of <strong>C</strong> gives a <strong>75%</strong> modifier. Losing reputation can cause you to lose force scale (but not rank).</p>
                <p class="mb-0">Winning a full contract (at least half the tracks) gains +1 reputation. You never lose reputation for losing a contract, only for breaching it (–3) or abandoning objectives.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Special Rules #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Special Rules</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <dl class="row mb-0">
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Commander</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Each force designates one Commander unit per track (written down, not revealed). No initiative penalty if the Commander is destroyed.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Forced Withdrawal</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Not in effect for player-generated forces. Crippled units may not perform pickup or scan actions. Forces without a company roster (opposing force table units) do use Forced Withdrawal.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Raid</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Mechs carrying Jump MP components must make a PSR at +3 or lose the components. All units carrying components have Run/Flank MP reduced by 1.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Scanning</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">A unit within 2 hexes (AS 4") may declare a scan instead of firing. Roll 2d6 in the End Phase — 7+ succeeds. Modifiers: +2 (no physical attack), +2 (Active Probe within 2 hexes), +2 (started within 2 hexes), –2 (used jump/VTOL MP), –2 (fired weapons, unless carrying unjammed Active Probe). A successful scan forces the defender to reveal relevant unit information.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Optional Rules #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Optional Rules</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Used by mutual agreement. Veteran players should defer to less experienced players. Assumed active for MRC games:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Floating Critical Hits (BMM p. 45 / TO:AR p. 75)</li>
                    <li>Careful Stand (BMM p. 19 / TO:AR p. 22)</li>
                    <li>Skin-of-the-teeth Ejection (BMM p. 81 / TO:AR p. 165)</li>
                    <li>One-Armed Prone Fire (BMM p. 30 / TO:AR p. 83)</li>
                    <li>ECCM (TO:AR p. 98–99)</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="mb-0"><strong>Special Munitions</strong> and Command Consoles may also be used by agreement.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Vehicles and Infantry #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Vehicles &amp; Infantry</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <dl class="row mb-0">
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Full Units</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">On the company roster with a pilot/crew. Pay all normal repair and training costs. May be salvaged. Conventional infantry is purchased in "barracks" — one barracks provides one unit per contract.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">Support Units</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Available at any track without being on the roster. Must be from the Periphery MUL, have no AE weapons, and be non-Mech/non-aerospace. Always 4/5 skill. Cannot be salvaged. Max 500 BV × scale.</dd>
                    <dt class="col-sm-4">BSAs</dt>
                    <dd class="col-sm-8">Battlefield Support Assets — optional for in-person or Tabletop Simulator play. Cannot be used alongside Support Units in the same track.</dd>
                </dl>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Time Between Tracks #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Time Between Tracks — SP Activity Costs</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-3">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Activity</th><th>Cost</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Armor damage only)</td><td>Tonnage × 0.5 (IS) / × 0.75 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Structure/Critical damage)</td><td>Tonnage × 2 (IS) / × 3.5 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Crippled)</td><td>Tonnage × 3 (IS) / × 4.5 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Repair (Destroyed)</td><td>Tonnage × 5 (IS) / × 7.5 (Mixed/Clan)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Reconfigure Unit</td><td>Tonnage / 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Purchase Mech</td><td>Battle Value (Point Value × 40)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Sell a Mech</td><td>Battle Value / 2 (Point Value × 20)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Rearm (per ton of ammo)</td><td>10 (20 for special ammo)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hire Unnamed MechWarrior/Crew</td><td>100</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Hire Named MechWarrior/Crew</td><td>150</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Heal Pilot</td><td>30 per Wound box (2 per month for AS)</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Heal/Replace BA Trooper</td><td>20 per trooper lost</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>Clan Doctrine Training</td><td>200</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Repair Time:</strong> Repairing a unit makes it unavailable for the rest of the month.</li>
                    <li><strong>Truly Destroyed:</strong> A unit is truly destroyed only if its center torso internal structure is eliminated — it is gone permanently.</li>
                    <li><strong>Named Pilots:</strong> Max 4 on roster. Earn XP (1 SP per XP, up to 200 XP × scale per track). MVP of each track earns +20 bonus XP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Unnamed Pilots:</strong> Always cost 100 SP to hire, always Gunnery 4 / Piloting 5, cannot earn XP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Handicap:</strong> Each named pilot has a handicap based on skill improvements. The player with the lower average handicap earns a BSP bonus equal to the handicap difference × 10.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Unit Handicap Table #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Unit Handicap Table</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Each named pilot has a handicap based on their Gunnery and Piloting/Anti-Mech skill levels. The player with the lower average handicap earns a BSP bonus equal to the handicap difference × 10. Unnamed pilots in a unit of 500 BV or more count toward the average handicap as if they were 0 handicap.</p>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr><th>Skill</th><th>Gunnery</th><th>Piloting / Anti-Mech</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>5</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>4</td><td>0</td><td>4</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>3</td><td>12</td><td>8</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>2</td><td>28</td><td>28</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>1</td><td>48</td><td>48</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>0</td><td>88</td><td>88</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Debt & Breaking Contract #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Debt &amp; Breaking Contract</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p><strong>Debt:</strong> If you can't pay maintenance or transport, you may go into debt. While in debt, SP Activities and unit training are suspended; all SP earned goes toward clearing the debt. A force cannot be in debt for more than 6 league months.</p>
                <p>While in debt you may: sell assets, retire your force, or skip one track (–1 reputation, available once while in debt).</p>
                <p><strong>Breaking Contract:</strong> Breaking a contract incurs a –3 reputation penalty (–1 if force is below 2/3 of the scale BV limit). The contract ends immediately — no more base pay, support, or transport coverage. The other player gains their reputation bonus for completing.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Big Leagues #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Big Leagues</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Big Leagues lets you join special mercenary commands with unique requirements, benefits, and purchase options. Available factions:</p>

                <h6 class="mt-3 mb-2">21st Centauri Lancers</h6>
                <ul class="mb-2">
                    <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> No broken contracts, no piracy, Scale 2+, Reputation 3+, Esprit de Corps Special Command</li>
                    <li><strong>Benefits:</strong> Treated as one higher Reputation in negotiations; half-cost Formations (Night Specialization, Environmental Specialization Clear, Ground Attack)</li>
                    <li><strong>Purchase Options:</strong> Crossbow T (1,300 SP), BattleMaster C (1,700 SP), Rifleman C 2 (1,200 SP), Hunchback IIC 5 (1,000 SP), LRM Carrier C (1,200 SP), SRM Carrier C (1,200 SP), Vedette Medium Tank V9 (1,000 SP), Condor Heavy Hover Tank Ultra (1,000 SP)</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="mb-2">Shadowblacks</h6>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Requirements:</strong> Must have participated in piracy, Scale 2+, 500 SP initiation fee</li>
                    <li><strong>Benefits:</strong> 20% bonus to piracy loot sale value; half-cost Formations (Striker Lance, Pursuit Lance); Clan tech upgrade option (Atlas C, Victor C, Marauder C, Warhammer C, Archer C, Thunderbolt C, Rifleman C, Shadow Hawk C, Phoenix Hawk C, Locust C)</li>
                    <li><strong>Clan Conversions:</strong> See individual unit SP costs in the rulebook</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Taking One For The Team #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Taking One For The Team (Overtime) — Full Mechanics</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>When contracts for the month are complete, players may run additional contracts with a new mercenary company. Regular military commands (House/Clan) can also use this option instead of standard mercenary play:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Reserve Forces:</strong> House/Clan forces get +50% BV/PV for reserve forces (e.g., Scale 1: 3,000 BV + 1,500 BV reserve).</li>
                    <li><strong>Support Points:</strong> Regular military commands get 300 SP per Scale (not a WarChest).</li>
                    <li><strong>No Negotiation:</strong> Regular military commands have no contract terms or negotiation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Promotion:</strong> 0–2 Reputation = Scale 1 (Sergeant); 3–10 = Scale 2 (Lieutenant); 11–20 = Scale 3 (Captain).</li>
                    <li><strong>Losing Reputation:</strong> Can cause loss of force scale (but not rank).</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Tracks Summary #}
    <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="card h-100">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Available Tracks</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Tracks are the individual battles within a contract. The primary player rolls to determine which track is played each month. All tracks end when one side has no units in play, or at the turn limit.</p>
                <ul class="mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Straight Fight</strong> — Destroy &gt;50% of the opponent's force. Classic engagement.</li>
                    <li><strong>Crush the Head</strong> — Target the enemy commander. Capture for maximum VP.</li>
                    <li><strong>Secure</strong> — Control the central objective point.</li>
                    <li><strong>Ambush</strong> — Defender hides units; attacker must scan objective hexes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Evacuation</strong> — Attacker extracts VIPs; Defender holds the line.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reconnaissance</strong> — Attacker scans enemy units and withdraws.</li>
                    <li><strong>Reinforce</strong> — Attacker must break through and exit the Defender's edge.</li>
                    <li><strong>Duel</strong> — One-on-one fight; interference by other units is penalised.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bounding Retreat</strong> — Defender holds then withdraws in good order; Attacker routs them.</li>
                    <li><strong>Calamity</strong> — Both sides deploy hidden; ECM is doubled, active probes disabled.</li>
                    <li><strong>Demolition</strong> — Attacker plants demolition charges on objective buildings.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# Contract Structure #}
    <div class="col-12">
        <div class="card">
            <div class="card-header bg-dark text-white"><h5 class="mb-0">Contract Structure &amp; Terms</h5></div>
            <div class="card-body">
                <p>Every contract is defined by <strong>five terms</strong>, each assigned a <strong>step</strong> on a 13-step table (steps 1–13, with step 20 as a special high-value tier). Higher steps mean better terms for the mercenary company. Each term is rolled independently on 2d6, with possible modifiers from the employer, affiliation, and contract type. The final step is clamped to the range 1–13.</p>

                <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                    <table class="table table-sm table-bordered">
                        <thead class="table-secondary">
                            <tr>
                                <th style="width:60px">Step</th>
                                <th>Base Pay</th>
                                <th>Command Rights</th>
                                <th>Salvage Rights</th>
                                <th>Support Terms</th>
                                <th>Transportation</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td>55%</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Straight / 20%</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>70%</td><td>—</td><td>10%</td><td>Straight / 60%</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>90%</td><td>—</td><td>30%</td><td>Straight / 100%</td><td>25%</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>8</strong></td><td>110%</td><td>Liaison</td><td>50%</td><td>Battle / 20%</td><td>75%</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>10</strong></td><td>130%</td><td>—</td><td>70%</td><td>Battle / 40%</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>12</strong></td><td>175%</td><td>—</td><td>90%</td><td>Battle / 75%</td><td>—</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><strong>20</strong></td><td colspan="5" class="text-center">Special high-value contract (negotiated terms)</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <div class="row g-4">
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <h6 class="mb-3">Base Pay</h6>
                        <p>The base monthly payment is calculated as <code>500 SP × contract scale × (basePayPercent / 100)</code>. For example, a scale-2 contract at step 4 (70%) pays <code>500 × 2 × 0.70 = 700 SP/month</code>. Base pay is paid per month of contract duration, regardless of how many tracks are completed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <h6 class="mb-3">Command Rights</h6>
                        <p>Command rights determine how much authority the mercenary company has over allied forces, and modify Command Complication rolls:</p>
                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li><strong>Integrated</strong> (+3) — Full command authority over allies.</li>
                            <li><strong>House</strong> (+2) — High authority, common with noble employers.</li>
                            <li><strong>Liaison</strong> (+1) — Advisory role; default when no command rights are specified.</li>
                            <li><strong>Independent</strong> (+0) — No authority over allies.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <h6 class="mb-3">Salvage Rights</h6>
                        <p>Salvage rights determine what percentage of a destroyed or crippled enemy unit's value the mercenary company is entitled to. Values range from <strong>Exchange</strong> (no salvage rights, the employer takes everything) to <strong>90%</strong>. If salvage rights are "Exchange," acquisition of salvaged mechs is not allowed. At 0% (None), acquisition is also disallowed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <h6 class="mb-3">Support Terms</h6>
                        <p>Support terms reduce the cost of repairing and maintaining your force:</p>
                        <ul class="mb-0">
                            <li><strong>Straight / X%</strong> — Reduces all repair costs by X%. At 100%, repairs are free.</li>
                            <li><strong>Battle / X%</strong> — Provides a support point payout equal to X% of a destroyed unit's value, compensating for the loss of the unit.</li>
                            <li><strong>None</strong> — No support benefits.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <h6 class="mb-3">Transportation Terms</h6>
                        <p>Transportation covers the cost of moving your force to and from the contract location. The cost is <code>300 SP × scale per journey</code>. The percentage listed (e.g., 25%, 75%) indicates how much of that cost the contract covers. The remainder is paid by the mercenary company. A dash (—) means no transportation is provided.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-md-6">
                        <h6 class="mb-3">Contract Types &amp; Duration</h6>
                        <ul>
                            <li><strong>Raid</strong> — 3 months. Quick strike operations.</li>
                            <li><strong>Expedition</strong> — 6 months. Extended operations away from home.</li>
                            <li><strong>Garrison</strong> — 6 months. Defensive holding contracts.</li>
                            <li><strong>Retainer</strong> — 6 months. Standing force on call.</li>
                            <li><strong>Invasion</strong> — 6 months. Offensive push into enemy territory.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-4">
                    <h6 class="mb-3">Negotiations</h6>
                    <p>Before accepting a contract, you may negotiate its terms by spending reputation points (maximum <code>2 × scale</code>). You can shift any term up or down by one step on the table. For every 2 steps you sacrifice in one term, you may gain 1 step in another — this exchange can be used <strong>twice</strong> per contract. Number of Tracks (1–5) is negotiated separately and is not on the step table.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
